    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6d6d4133-7ac4-5f00-91cf-e56a1ff09a1b",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
